// Package schemas validates go-mutants JSON documents against the schemas
// embedded in the binary.
//
// One function does the work:
//
//	err := schemas.Validate(schemas.CatalogV1, doc)
//
// The caller names the document type it believes it produced, hands over the
// encoded bytes, and gets back either nil or an [Error] carrying a stable
// GOM50xx code and the JSON pointer of the offending value.
//
// # Why validate our own output
//
// Every document go-mutants writes is generated by go-mutants, so validating
// it looks circular. It is not. The schemas are published — they are what a
// consumer writes their jq expression or their decoder against — and a
// published contract that the producer never checks is a contract that drifts
// the first time a field is renamed in one place and not the other. Validating
// on the way out turns that drift into a failing test in this repository
// instead of a broken pipeline in somebody else's CI.
//
// It also protects the one property that makes these documents worth reading:
// `additionalProperties: false` everywhere. A typo'd key is a bug, not a
// forward-compatible extension, and the only moment it is cheap to catch is
// before the file is written.
//
// # Compiled once, offline, always
//
// The schemas are compiled on first use and kept for the life of the process.
// Compilation is not free — it parses every schema and resolves every $ref —
// and a run validates one document per report format, so paying that cost per
// call would be pure waste. A failure to compile is remembered too: a schema
// that is broken now is broken for the rest of the process, and retrying it on
// every call would hide the failure behind a different error each time.
//
// Nothing is ever fetched. The documents come from the embedded [schema.FS],
// the draft 2020-12 meta-schema ships inside the validator, and no URL loader
// is registered — so a $ref to a URL this package does not already hold is a
// compile error rather than a network call. A mutation-testing tool that
// reaches the internet to check its own output would be both slow and
// surprising, and would fail in exactly the environments — sealed CI, air-gapped
// build hosts — that most need reproducibility.
//
// # Why unknown document types are an error
//
// [Validate] refuses a document type it has no schema for instead of passing
// the document through unchecked. The alternative — "no schema, no opinion" —
// makes a typo in a caller's document-type constant look like success, which is
// the one outcome validation exists to prevent. Adding a document type is one
// entry in the registry and one file in schema/.
//
// # The pointer is chosen deterministically
//
// A validation failure reports one location, and which one is not left to
// chance. The validator returns a tree of errors whose branches follow map
// iteration order, so "the first error in the tree" differs between runs of the
// same program on the same input. This package instead collects every leaf of
// that tree and picks the smallest by (instance pointer, keyword), which is a
// total order over a set that does not depend on iteration order. Two runs over
// the same invalid document therefore blame the same field, which is what makes
// the error greppable, testable, and quotable in a bug report.
//
// Missing and unexpected properties are reported at the property, not at the
// object containing it: the validator locates both at the parent object, and
// "the document is invalid at /mutants/0" is a much worse answer than
// "/mutants/0/line" when the object has thirteen fields.
package schemas
